//! lotus/stereo_sweep.rye -- ALES175: a FILTER SWEEP carried into stereo -- ALES44's moving-cutoff low-pass run on BOTH channels with the SAME schedule (num_from -> num_to over den, in blocks blocks), the sixth rung of the stereo EQ /
//! filter class and the first AUTOMATED stereo parameter. The class holds its tone control (ALES170), shelf (ALES171), band-pass (ALES172, isolate the middle), notch (ALES173, drop the middle), and bell (ALES174, turn the middle);
//! every one of those holds ONE brightness for a whole span. The gesture a musician reaches for as often as a fixed cut is the SWEEP -- a cutoff that MOVES across the span, the filter opening or closing over a phrase (the slow open
//! on a pad, the close into a drop). ALES44 built that for a mono Clip: it partitions the span into blocks, runs ALES43's carried-state low-pass over each with a coefficient interpolated linearly from num_from to num_to, and threads
//! ONE state across every block, so a boundary never re-transients and a degenerate sweep (num_from == num_to) equals ALES40's fixed low_pass over the whole span byte for byte. This rung carries it into stereo the way the EQ class
//! asks -- run ALES44's proven mono `sweep_low_pass` on each channel with the SAME schedule, so the moving brightness opens on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! Like every parametric edit (ALES171's shelf, ALES174's bell), a sweep's endpoints and block count are values the caller NAMES, not scalars measured across the field, so the SAME schedule on each channel preserves the stereo
//! image for free -- no linking, no measurement. Each channel sweeps its OWN content, which is exactly right for a filter: an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical because the same input through the same schedule
//! is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. UNLIKE the stateless shelf, notch, and bell, the sweep DOES carry filter state -- yet each channel opens its OWN `state = 0` inside its
//! own call and threads it across its OWN blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory (a real stereo filter never leaks one side's history into the other) and there is no cross-channel state to thread across a seam.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadCoeff (an endpoint outside (0, 1]), BadRange (a span past the shared length), and BadBlocks (a zero, over-max, or empty-block partition) BEFORE either channel is touched -- the two endpoints, the
//! span, and the block count validated once against the shared length up front (ALES44's own `check_sweep`, exported for this), so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the other torn; then run ALES44's `sweep_low_pass` on
//! each channel with the SAME schedule. A degenerate schedule (num_from == num_to) is exactly ALES40's fixed low_pass on both; a settled constant sweeps to itself on both (a constant is a fixed point of every legal coefficient).
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE STEREO SWEEP LAW: each channel equals ALES44's mono `sweep_low_pass` with the SAME schedule over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content -- the left
//! swept by the left's spectrum, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE DEGENERATE LAW: a degenerate stereo sweep (num_from == num_to) over any block partition equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on BOTH channels byte for byte (the
//! carried state makes the partition invisible per channel), and a settled constant sweeps to itself on both. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master stays identical through the sweep (a mono-in-stereo signal never splits), the
//! shared schedule holding the image. THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an all-silent master sweeps to silence on both; left.len == right.len after; an illegal endpoint, an out-of-range span, and an illegal block
//! count each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES175.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-081412_fill-ales175-lotus-stereo-sweep.md
//! Stands on: lotus/sweep.rye (ALES44) -- the mono filter sweep run once per channel, its SweepError reused whole and its `check_sweep` reused for the up-front atomic validation; lotus/tone.rye (ALES40, ALES43) -- the fixed and
//! carried-state low-pass the sweep interpolates between, the degenerate-law equality target; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each swept through ALES44's own bounded block partition (max_sweep_blocks = 4096), the filter state at full i64 precision per channel,
//! siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The coefficient still smooths over sample INDICES, now under a schedule; a cutoff in hertz is the later time-base rung. No real
//! sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_sweep.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const tone = @import("tone.rye");
const sweep = @import("sweep.rye");

pub const stereo_sweep_version = "20260815.081412";

/// The stereo sweep's faults are exactly ALES44's own `SweepError` -- `BadCoeff` on an illegal endpoint, `BadRange` on a span outside the samples, and `BadBlocks` on an illegal partition -- reused by name, since carrying the sweep
/// into stereo adds no new fault: it validates one shared schedule against the shared length, then runs the same mono sweep on each channel.
pub const StereoSweepError = sweep.SweepError;

/// Sweep a low-pass over `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME schedule -- `num_from -> num_to` over `den`, partitioned into `blocks` blocks -- by running ALES44's proven mono
/// `sweep_low_pass` on each channel. Each channel opens its OWN i64 filter state and threads it across its OWN blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory; the shared schedule preserves the stereo image. A degenerate
/// schedule (num_from == num_to) equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on both; a settled constant sweeps to itself on both. Validates the two endpoints, the span, and the block count ONCE against the shared length before either channel
/// is mutated (ALES44's `check_sweep`): refuses BadCoeff, BadRange, and BadBlocks, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_sweep(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    num_from: i32,
    num_to: i32,
    den: u32,
    blocks: u32,
) StereoSweepError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared schedule preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the schedule, span, and partition are legal against the SHARED length -- checked before any write, so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try sweep.check_sweep(len, start, count, num_from, num_to, den, blocks);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same schedule, same span, shared length), so each mono sweep is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME schedule on
    // each channel, each channel carrying its own filter state across its own blocks.
    try sweep.sweep_low_pass(&sc.left, start, count, num_from, num_to, den, blocks);
    try sweep.sweep_low_pass(&sc.right, start, count, num_from, num_to, den, blocks);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- a sweep writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SWEEP LAW -- each channel equals ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass with the SAME schedule byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE DEGENERATE LAW -- a degenerate sweep equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on
//     both, a settled constant sweeping to itself; THE IMAGE LAW -- an identical-channel master staying identical; THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- silence to silence, balanced, each refusal untouched, count 0
//     identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO SWEEP LAW -- each channel equals ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass with the SAME schedule, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left swept by the left's spectrum, the
    //    right by the right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000, 1000, 500, -900, 1100 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500, 900, -800, 1200, 900 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_sweep(&sc, 0, 12, 1, 4, 4, 6); // 1/4 → 4/4 over four, in six blocks, on both

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try sweep.sweep_low_pass(&ml, 0, 12, 1, 4, 4, 6);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try sweep.sweep_low_pass(&mr, 0, 12, 1, 4, 4, 6);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftSweepNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightSweepNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ -- the left is not the right swept, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2a. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- a degenerate stereo sweep (num_from == num_to) over any block partition equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on BOTH channels byte for byte. The carried state makes the block partition invisible per
    //     channel, so a still schedule adds no arithmetic to either audio path.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 1000, -800, 1200, 900, -1100, 700, 1300, -600, 1000, 500, -900, 1100 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500, 900, -800, 1200, 900 };
        var swept = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&swept, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_sweep(&swept, 0, 12, 1, 1, 3, 4); // 1/3 → 1/3 in four blocks — degenerate

        var fl = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&fl, 0, &ll);
        try tone.low_pass(&fl, 0, 12, 1, 3);
        var fr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&fr, 0, &rr);
        try tone.low_pass(&fr, 0, 12, 1, 3);

        if (!eq(swept.left.samples(), fl.samples())) return error.LeftDegenerateNotFixed;
        if (!eq(swept.right.samples(), fr.samples())) return error.RightDegenerateNotFixed;
    }

    // 2b. a settled constant sweeps to itself on both channels -- a constant is a fixed point of every legal coefficient and the carried state never restarts, so the swept tail equals the input exactly, per channel.
    {
        const run = [_]i16{2500} ** 48;
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &run, &run);
        try stereo_sweep(&sc, 0, 48, 1, 2, 2, 6); // 1/2 → 2/2 in six blocks
        if (sc.left.samples()[47] != 2500 or sc.right.samples()[47] != 2500) return error.ConstantNotSettled;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW -- an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the sweep: the same input through the same schedule is the same deterministic output, so the two channels never split.
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300, 1200, -1200, 600, -600 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_sweep(&sc, 0, 12, 1, 4, 4, 6); // moving brightness
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 4a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master sweeps to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_sweep(&sc, 0, 6, 1, 3, 4, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 100, 50 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, -50, 60 });
        try stereo_sweep(&bc, 0, 6, 1, 2, 2, 3);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 6) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- an illegal endpoint (num_to above den -- unstable), an out-of-range span, and an illegal block count (more blocks than samples -- an empty block) each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte
    //     untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_sweep(&a, 0, 3, 1, 3, 2, 3)) |_| return error.BadCoeffSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadCoeff;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.CoeffRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_sweep(&b, 1, 3, 1, 2, 2, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_sweep(&c, 0, 3, 1, 2, 2, 4)) |_| return error.BadBlocksSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadBlocks) return error.WrongBadBlocks;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.BlocksRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to sweep.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_sweep(&noop, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 1); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-sweep: a FILTER SWEEP CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES44's moving-cutoff low-pass run on BOTH channels with the SAME schedule (num_from to num_to over den, in blocks blocks), the sixth rung of the stereo EQ " ++
        "/ filter class and the first AUTOMATED stereo parameter. The class holds its tone control (ALES170), shelf (ALES171), band-pass (ALES172), notch (ALES173), and bell (ALES174), each holding ONE brightness for a span; the " ++
        "sweep MOVES the cutoff across the span (the slow open on a pad, the close into a drop). ALES44 partitions the span into blocks, runs a carried-state low-pass over each with a coefficient interpolated num_from to num_to, and " ++
        "threads ONE state across every block so a boundary never re-transients, so a degenerate sweep (num_from == num_to) equals ALES40's fixed low_pass over the whole span byte for byte. A sweep's endpoints and block count are " ++
        "values the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same schedule on each channel preserves the stereo image for free (no linking, no measurement); each channel sweeps its own content, and unlike the " ++
        "stateless shelf, notch, and bell the sweep DOES carry filter state — yet each channel opens its OWN state and threads it across its OWN blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory. It validates the two " ++
        "endpoints, the span, and the block count once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff, BadRange, BadBlocks; ALES44's check_sweep reused), so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the " ++
        "other torn; SweepError reused whole. THE STEREO SWEEP LAW: each channel equals ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass with the same schedule byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing. THE DEGENERATE LAW: a degenerate sweep " ++
        "equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on both byte for byte, a settled constant sweeping to itself on both. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical through the sweep, the shared schedule holding the image. THE " ++
        "BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: silence staying silence on both, left.len == right.len after, an illegal endpoint / an out-of-range span / an illegal block count each refusing by name with both channels " ++
        "untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips swept through the proven mono sweep's bounded block partition, the filter state at full i64 precision per channel, the coefficient " ++
        "over sample indices not hertz; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
